/// Datasheet for the `text` device — a DUMB text-display library. It renders a SCREEN-SPACE text run that the
/// DRIVER hands it; it owns no text logic. What to say, where in the WORLD, how it projects to the screen,
/// parallax, layout — all live in the driver. The device only turns a projected run into pixels or DOM.
///
/// A backend `render` system reads the driver-provided display-run columns (the driver declares these types
/// and puts them on its own archetype, exactly as gfx's `rect` reads the driver's `rx/ry/rw/rh/rcolor`):
///   tx, ty  — the run's TOP-LEFT in RENDER pixels (already projected by the driver)
///   tstr    — a fixed `[N]char` buffer holding the text; tlen — its live char count
///   tsz     — pixel height of the text; tcol — colour 0xRRGGBB
///
/// Backends (interchangeable variants of this ONE lib; pick with `[select] text = "..."`, never both):
///   framebuffer — blits a bitmap font into the driver's `{ framebuffer }` (shared, DRIVER-owned, the same
///                 buffer gfx renders into), so text composites into the image on EVERY gfx target (native
///                 window, web canvas, headless). No host seam.
///   dom         — emits one positioned <span> per run over the web canvas (real browser fonts); the JS host
///                 (www/text.js) fulfils its `text_be_*` seams.
///
/// A HUD label is just a run whose `tx/ty` the driver seeds fixed (no projection); a world sign is one whose
/// `tx/ty` the driver fills each frame from a world anchor via `camera.to_screen`. The device can't tell them
/// apart — it only ever draws the projected run.
// RUN: true
